The FilePermission class in the java.io package represents permission to access a file or directory using operations such as reading, writing, executing, deleting, and reading symbolic links. It was designed for Java's former Security Manager permission model.FilePermission is deprecated for removal since Java 25.It is now mainly useful for understanding and maintaining legacy Java applications that used the Security Manager.Note: The Security Manager is no longer supported. Therefore, FilePermission should not be used as a modern mechanism for restricting file-system access.
            Java

    import java.io.FilePermission;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FilePermission fp = new FilePermission("GEEKS.txt", "read");
        System.out.println(fp.getActions());
    }
}

Output:readExplanation:The FilePermission object is created with the action "read".The getActions() method returns the canonical string representing the allowed actions.Since only one action is specified, it simply prints read.FilePermissionFilePermission is a final class that extends java.security.Permission. A FilePermission object contains:A file or directory path.One or more actions associated with that path.For example:FilePermission permission = new FilePermission("GEEKS.txt", "read,write");This object represents read and write permissions for GEEKS.txt.However, these objects no longer provide a supported mechanism for enforcing file access in current Java versions because the Security Manager functionality has been discontinued. Supported ActionsA FilePermission object can represent the following file-access actions:read: Represents permission to read a file or directory.write: Represents permission to write to or modify a file.execute: Represents permission to execute a file.delete: Represents permission to delete a file or directory.readlink: Represents permission to read the target of a symbolic link.Class Declaration of FilePermission Classpublic final class FilePermission extends Permission implements SerializableConstructorCreates a new FilePermission object with the specified actions.FilePermission(String path, String actions)Parameters:path: The file or directory path.actions: Actions to be allowed (e.g., "read", "write").Methods of FilePermission ClassImportant methods provided by FilePermission include:1. equals(Object obj)Used to compare two FilePermission objects for equality. It returns true only when both the file path and the permitted actions are exactly the same.Syntax:public boolean equals(Object obj)Parameters: obj - the FilePermission object to compare.Returns: true if equal, otherwise false.
            Java

    import java.io.FilePermission;

public class EqualsExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FilePermission fp1 = new FilePermission("GEEKS.txt", "read");
        FilePermission fp2 = new FilePermission("GEEKS.txt", "read");

        System.out.println(fp1.equals(fp2)); // Output: true
    }
}

Outputtrue
2. getActions() Provides a standardized (canonical) string of all actions granted to the permission. The actions are returned in a fixed order and separated by commas.Syntax:public String getActions()Returns: Canonical string: representing the actions associated with the object.
            Java

    import java.io.FilePermission;

public class GetActionsExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FilePermission fp = new FilePermission("GEEKS.txt", "read,write");
        System.out.println(fp.getActions()); // Output: read,write
    }
}

Outputread,write
3. hashCode() Generates a hash value that represents the FilePermission object. This value is consistent with equals() and is useful when permissions are stored in hash-based collections.Syntax:public int hashCode()Returns: Returns the hash code value for the FilePermission object.
            Java

    import java.io.FilePermission;

public class HashCodeExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FilePermission fp = new FilePermission("GEEKS.txt", "read");
        System.out.println(fp.hashCode()); // Output: some integer hash value
    }
}

Output-989797644
Note: The exact hash code should not be treated as a fixed value.4. implies(Permission arg)Determines whether the current permission fully covers another permission. It returns true if the current object allows all actions requested by the given permission.Syntax:public boolean implies(Permission p)Parameters:p - the permission to check.Returns:true if the permission is implied, otherwise false.
            Java

    import java.io.FilePermission;
import java.security.Permission;

public class ImpliesExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FilePermission fp1 = new FilePermission("GEEKS.txt", "read,write");
        FilePermission fp2 = new FilePermission("GEEKS.txt", "read");

        System.out.println(fp1.implies(fp2)); // Output: true
    }
}

Outputtrue
5. newPermissionCollection()The newPermissionCollection() method creates a PermissionCollection designed for storing FilePermission objects. It is primarily relevant to the legacy permission model associated with Java's former Security Manager architecture.Syntax:public PermissionCollection newPermissionCollection()Returns: A PermissionCollection that can store FilePermission objects.
            Java

    import java.io.FilePermission;
import java.security.PermissionCollection;

public class PermissionCollectionExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FilePermission fp1 = new FilePermission("GEEKS.txt", "read");
        FilePermission fp2 = new FilePermission("GEEKS.txt", "write");

        PermissionCollection pc = fp1.newPermissionCollection();
        pc.add(fp1);
        pc.add(fp2);

        System.out.println(pc.elements().hasMoreElements()); // Output: true
    }
}

Outputtrue
